id":"MDQ6VXNlcjMyOTEyNjU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3291265?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/lezcano","html_url":"https://github.com/lezcano","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lezcano/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lezcano/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lezcano/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lezcano/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lezcano/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lezcano/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lezcano/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lezcano/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/lezcano/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2023-05-01T13:09:24Z","updated_at":"2023-05-01T13:09:24Z","body":"Ooops, no it's not. It's fixed just for CUDA for now.","author_association":"COLLABORATOR","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/comments/1529696345/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/comments/1536986127","html_url":"https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/issues/100343#issuecomment-1536986127","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/100343","id":1536986127,"node_id":"IC_kwDOA-j9z85bnIwP","user":{"login":"Valentine233","id":23010269,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIzMDEwMjY5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/23010269?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Valentine233","html_url":"https://github.com/Valentine233","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Valentine233/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Valentine233/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Valentine233/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Valentine233/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Valentine233/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Valentine233/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Valentine233/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Valentine233/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Valentine233/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2023-05-06T02:13:03Z","updated_at":"2023-05-06T02:14:33Z","body":"The issue is about accessing illegal memory when doing indexing `x[idx]` with invalid indices `idx`. As the real value of `idx` is unknown until running cpp output code, the validation checking need to be done in cpp code. For triton, it adopts `device_asserts` (https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/pull/98590). For cpp, I suppose maybe to generate a specific kernel for such checking, but I am not clear about the details now. Do you have any idea about the solution? cc @jgong5 @EikanWang ","author_association":"COLLABORATOR","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/comments/1536986127/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/comments/1537043457","html_url":"https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/issues/100343#issuecomment-1537043457","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/100343","id":1537043457,"node_id":"IC_kwDOA-j9z85bnWwB","user":{"login":"jgong5","id":8359223,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgzNTkyMjM=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/8359223?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jgong5","html_url":"https://github.com/jgong5","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jgong5/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jgong5/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jgong5/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jgong5/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jgong5/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jgong5/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jgong5/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jgong5/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jgong5/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2023-05-06T04:27:14Z","updated_at":"2023-05-06T04:27:33Z","body":"> The issue is about accessing illegal memory when doing indexing `x[idx]` with invalid indices `idx`. As the real value of `idx` is unknown until running cpp output code, the validation checking need to be done in cpp code. For triton, it adopts `device_asserts` (#98590). For cpp, I suppose maybe to generate a specific kernel for such checking, but I am not clear about the details now. Do you have any idea about the solution? cc @jgong5 @EikanWang\r\n\r\nThe places to add assertion would be similar to #98590 (in `ops.load` and `ops.store` functions) while you may consider to refactor the code a bit to reuse the logic in `gen_assert_indirect_indexing` which currently contains two parts: 1) retrieve the size of the indirect indexing which is common for both cpp and triton and can be moved to `common.py`; 2) generate assertion line for triton, which can be left in `triton.py` while for cpp, we add something in parallel in `cpp.py` which generates assertion in c++, e.g., invoking `TORCH_CHECK`.","author_association":"COLLABORATOR","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytorch/pytorch/issues/comments/1537043457/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"perform